QCL (Quantum Computation Language)
QCL (Quantum Computation Language) is one of the first quantum programming languages, with a C-type syntax. It allows combining quantum code and classical code in the same program.
Example — simulation of a coin toss:
procedure FlipCoin() {
  qureg q[1]; int x;
  reset;
  H(q);
  measure q, x;
  if x == 1 { print "Heads"; }
  if x == 0 { print "Tails"; }
  reset;
}
In this example:

H(q) applies the Hadamard gate to the quantum register q, creating a perfect superposition.
measure q, x measures the qubit and stores the result in the classic variable x.
